  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21727v1</id>
    <title>Your Dense Retriever is Secretly an Expeditious Reasoner</title>
    <updated>2025-09-27T16:50:03Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21727v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.21727v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Dense retrievers enhance retrieval by encoding queries and documents into continuous vectors, but they often struggle with reasoning-intensive queries. Although Large Language Models (LLMs) can reformulate queries to capture complex reasoning, applying them universally incurs significant computational cost. In this work, we propose Adaptive Query Reasoning (AdaQR), a hybrid query rewriting framework. Within this framework, a Reasoner Router dynamically directs each query to either fast dense reasoning or deep LLM reasoning. The dense reasoning is achieved by the Dense Reasoner, which performs LLM-style reasoning directly in the embedding space, enabling a controllable trade-off between efficiency and accuracy. Experiments on large-scale retrieval benchmarks BRIGHT show that AdaQR reduces reasoning cost by 28% while preserving-or even improving-retrieval performance by 7%.</summary>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-27T16:50:03Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>16 pages, 11 figures</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.IR"/>
    <author>
      <name>Yichi Zhang</name>
